WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.800
What would happen if somebody just handed you&nbsp;
three strange magic cards and asked you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.760
build a commander deck around them? Could you do&nbsp;
it? Recently I asked my patrons what cards they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.800
wanted to see me play in a deck, and then I ran a&nbsp;
poll to decide the three I’d be building with. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.520
results? The Sibsig Ceremony, Aether Vial, and&nbsp;
Birthing Ritual. This is actually not the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.440
set of cards I could have gotten handed to me.&nbsp;
The Sibsig Ceremony was the card that by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.760
most people most wanted to see, and this Tarkir:&nbsp;
Dragonstorm enchantment makes all of our creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.280
cost two colorless less but destroys them when&nbsp;
they enter if we cast them, replacing them with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.640
a zombie. This is definitely a big downside, but&nbsp;
the silver lining here is that the other two cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.280
offer ways to get creatures into play without&nbsp;
casting them. Birthing Ritual is a two mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.680
enchantment that offers a sort of limited birthing&nbsp;
pod effect, letting us look at the top 7 cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.560
of our library on each end step and sacrifice a&nbsp;
creature to replace it with one from those cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.360
Aether Vial is a much older card, a one mana&nbsp;
artifact that can gain a counter on our upkeep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.000
and which taps to put a creature with mana value&nbsp;
equal to the number of counters on it into play.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.240
So, where to start here? For one,&nbsp;
we’re in at least Golgari colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.040
and with a triple black on The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;
we’re gonna be hard-pressed to push too far beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.000
that without some Urborg-oriented assistance.&nbsp;
All three of these cards care about creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.160
and some of them care about creatures kind&nbsp;
of a lot. Birthing Ritual is probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.720
lowest-commitment card here—it has a low mana&nbsp;
cost, relatively flexible criteria for podding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.760
things, and you get to see all your options before&nbsp;
sacrificing. Really the only thing this needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.160
work well is a moderately large creature count,&nbsp;
though it can also help to have a decent spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.960
between mana values to allow stairstepping up in&nbsp;
mana costs. Speaking of which, let’s talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.000
Aether Vial. It’s a card traditionally played in&nbsp;
heavily-creature-based decks with relatively low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.320
average mana values—typically you drop it&nbsp;
early, get it up to two or three counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.440
and then pop out extra dudes at instant speed for&nbsp;
the rest of the game. This makes sense in a fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.200
60 card deck, but what about in an EDH deck? The&nbsp;
effect of deploying creatures more rapidly isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.360
such a guaranteed benefit in the slower, typically&nbsp;
higher-curved EDH format. The card also doesn’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.960
anything immediately after played, which certainly&nbsp;
doesn’t spell success for a card in EDH, but maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.360
there’s a niche for it—it’s still a low-cost&nbsp;
card, and the instant speed might be useful in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.920
some instances. Like Birthing Ritual, this card&nbsp;
is going to be looking for a high creature count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.720
to be useful, and preferably a relatively low&nbsp;
average mana cost among those creatures so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.600
the card is actually able to feel impactful in&nbsp;
the first couple turns after it’s been played.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.000
The most challenging thing here is definitely&nbsp;
The Sibsig Ceremony, and that’s because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.320
comes with a fat downside—your creatures&nbsp;
literally get destroyed as soon as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.920
enter. Among the five commanders this sees&nbsp;
significant play in, none include green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.400
so we’re gonna have to get creative here. One of&nbsp;
the issues in commander selection is that even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.040
a commander works well once it’s on the board with&nbsp;
Sibsig in play, you’re constrained to playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.200
commander before the enchantment, and if your&nbsp;
commander ever gets removed Sibsig is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.520
to make it tricky to get it back onto the board.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say there are no solutions to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.680
though. Indestructible works since Sibsig&nbsp;
destroys the creatures rather than making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.280
you sacrifice them, and this leaves a handful&nbsp;
of options such as Sapling of Colfenor, Kotis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.400
most Gods, and any partner pair that includes&nbsp;
Anara. There are also other commanders that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.240
can be made to have Indestructible if you&nbsp;
build them right—the Mimeoplasm can copy a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.480
creature with indestructible, and Indoraptor&nbsp;
will likewise be indestructible if you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.720
an indestructible creature to discard. You&nbsp;
could also run some cards in the 99 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.680
regenerate a creature or return it to play&nbsp;
when it dies, pull a Scam on your opponents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.200
But what about the final option, the forbidden&nbsp;
option—what if your commander wants to die?

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.600
This brings me to my choice of champion—Iname&nbsp;
as One. This is a 12 mana 8/8 that, if played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.440
from hand, tutors out a spirit into play when it&nbsp;
enters, and recurs a spirit to play when it dies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.920
But what sorts of spirits might you want to tutor&nbsp;
and recur? Well, how about Iname and Iname? Here’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.800
the default plan when we cast our commander from&nbsp;
hand with The Sibsig Ceremony in play: Iname as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.920
One enters. Two triggers go on the stack, one to&nbsp;
destroy it, and the other to tutor out a spirit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.200
We choose to resolve the spirit-searching first,&nbsp;
and tutor out Iname, Death Aspect. When this card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.840
enters, it searches our library for any number of&nbsp;
spirits and puts them into our graveyard. We put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.280
whichever spirits we like into our graveyard,&nbsp;
with one of them being Iname, Life Aspect. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.640
the Sibsig trigger on Iname as One resolves and&nbsp;
it dies, creating a zombie, and then its death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.560
trigger happens, putting Iname, Life Aspect back&nbsp;
into play. Now what happens from here? Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.880
could be a lot of things, but ideally if&nbsp;
Life Aspect is indeed your choice to recur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.640
you’ve got some way or other to sacrifice&nbsp;
it. One of these would be Birthing Ritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.960
but there are several others. Illuminor&nbsp;
Szeras can sacrifice this Iname for mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.240
Greater Good can draw us some cards,&nbsp;
He Who Hungers can force an opponent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.520
to discard something, and so on, ten cards in&nbsp;
total. This might seem like a lot of sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.640
just to enable one single element of our plan,&nbsp;
but something I noticed early on in building is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.880
that sacrifice outlets are actually pretty&nbsp;
sweet with The Sibsig Ceremony in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.400
because it destroys the creatures. So you’ve got&nbsp;
all these creatures that are going to die anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:14.320
and you may as well sacrifice them. There are&nbsp;
even some that care about power or mana value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:21.200
and for those, sacrificing Iname as One itself can&nbsp;
be a fantastic power burst in an already big turn.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.080
In some sense this gameplan might seem like&nbsp;
a combo, but in truth, having as many of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.040
spirits in hand as it wants does not win the&nbsp;
game. It certainly makes it easier, and will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.600
lead to rapid victory sometimes with the right&nbsp;
prerequisites, but I didn’t feel like hammering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.040
this deck’s commander plan into a consistent win,&nbsp;
for reasons that may become clear a bit later. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.440
instead this deck is a patchwork of different&nbsp;
payoffs and wincons that align with the central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.200
pillars of this deck that we’ve laid out.&nbsp;
First off, there’s a perfectly adequate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.400
spirits midrange plan. A couple Myojin exist&nbsp;
as top-end indestructible threats, one of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.520
being Myojin of Life’s Web that can dump a loaded&nbsp;
hand onto the board in short order. There are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.600
several other spirits with effects that happen&nbsp;
when spirit or arcane cards are played—Infernal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.240
Kirin strips out opposing hands, Kodama of the&nbsp;
South Tree pumps up our board, zombies included,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.120
and Kodama of the East Tree can allow for quicker&nbsp;
deployment of spirits. Getting these out safely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.400
with The Sibsig Ceremony in play can be tricky,&nbsp;
but there are a few options. One would be to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.160
regenerate a creature with Accursed Duneyard,&nbsp;
and another would be to use the front side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.040
Malakir Rebirth or Revitalizing Repast to keep&nbsp;
the creature in question alive. The final option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.840
is Aether Vial—though this deck doesn’t fit the&nbsp;
conventional mana curve of an Aether Vial deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.320
it is at least somewhat predictable in what&nbsp;
spirits it will have available, and can plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.040
ahead to have a certain number of counters for a&nbsp;
pivotal turn based on what seems like it’ll be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.880
best play. The final option for a wincon here is&nbsp;
the lands package, and this actually came up about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.560
quite organically. First, a number of specific&nbsp;
lands really help out with our gameplan—Urborg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.960
for casting The Sibsig Ceremony, Reliquary&nbsp;
Tower for holding a fat hand of spirits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.440
Accursed Duneyard for getting around The Sibsig&nbsp;
Ceremony, Command Beacon for getting Iname into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.560
hand, and a few second-tier lands as well. In&nbsp;
addition, a number of cards that naturally fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.680
into the deck just inherently work well with&nbsp;
a lands package—Kura is a classic lands card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.880
Wight of the Reliquary is a lovely sac ability&nbsp;
zombie, and Spirit of the Aldergard is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.920
friendly bear who can find us a Dark Depths.&nbsp;
So herein lies the final wincon—Field of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.000
Dead can get us even more zombies, Dark&nbsp;
Depths can do its dark and depthy things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.640
and a couple manlands can swing real big&nbsp;
as a result of this deck’s strange quirks.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.600
So that’s how this deck tries to run to the finish&nbsp;
line, but it’d be a little unfair for me to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.400
talk about the setup steps. Like sure, for some&nbsp;
decks you can start the gameplan at casting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.800
commander, but getting to that point is in this&nbsp;
case a bit of a herculean task. The card is 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.240
mana, and relevantly we need to get it into our&nbsp;
hand before playing it—the ETB trigger doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.120
happen if we cast it from the command zone. So&nbsp;
let’s start there. In this deck there are four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.920
cards that allow getting our commander into&nbsp;
the command zone—Campfire, Netherborn Alter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.040
Road of Return, and Command Beacon—and getting&nbsp;
one of these will naturally be a requirement for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.720
any sort of commander based gameplay, unless we’re&nbsp;
real down bad for a recursion spell. Next up, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.240
12 mana part—that’s a lot of mana, but thankfully&nbsp;
there’s a decent amount of high caliber ramp that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:25.120
slots nicely into this deck. There are several&nbsp;
ramp sources that are spirits as well, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.360
include a handful of spirit-or-arcane-effect&nbsp;
cards from original Kamigawa, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.520
Crypt Ghast, which is a fantastic card if&nbsp;
we can get Urborg into play. Joining these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:38.400
is Illuminor Szeras and Soldevi Adnate, which&nbsp;
we’d prefer to use on one of the Inames to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.720
a massive surge of mana, but these can also help&nbsp;
build up to a commander cast as well in a pinch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.400
Finally there are a few other ramp cards that just&nbsp;
happen to be lovely in this particular deck, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:54.160
those including Enduring Vitality, Seance Board,&nbsp;
and the aforementioned Wight of the Reliquary.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:59.200
Joining these is a decent draw suite as well—14&nbsp;
cards if I include Birthing Ritual among them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.120
which I am choosing to do, and if you have issues&nbsp;
with that I recommend you leave a comment telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.520
me all about it. Many of these are quite&nbsp;
large scale draw, drawing potentially five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:12.160
or more cards given average circumstances, and&nbsp;
my hope is that pairing this with the similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.840
potent ramp suite should allow the deck to&nbsp;
consistently climb in mana and get where it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.800
needs to go. I’m also running four tutors to&nbsp;
find The Sibsig Ceremony or other key pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.760
which isn’t a massive number but should be enough&nbsp;
with the other draw present. In the ideal world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.400
I’d love to go a little bit higher with each of&nbsp;
these values, but one thing this deck doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:35.520
have a lot of is extra space to work with. I’m&nbsp;
already spending a bunch of slots just making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.240
my questionable commander even functional, and&nbsp;
adding more draw and ramp would inevitably end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.560
up eating into something else. My amount of&nbsp;
interaction is already lower than I’d like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.040
so the only way to make more space would be to&nbsp;
cut spirits, which would weaken my spirit synergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.160
cards and give me fewer options once I’m doing&nbsp;
Iname-oriented things. Ultimately, the version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:59.040
of the list I have right now seems decently enough&nbsp;
balanced between its different goals—able to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.840
as a sort of aristocrats-y spirit midrange deck,&nbsp;
but also able to make some big plays with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.240
commander once a handful of different conditions&nbsp;
are met. It could be cool to do a variant on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.800
deck that emphasizes a different part of the&nbsp;
gameplan more heavily, but with this deck I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:17.200
think I’ve constructed a pretty reasonable,&nbsp;
middle of the road approach. However, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.320
process left me wondering what else could be done&nbsp;
with these three cards I’d built a deck around.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.480
So, I challenged a few other content creators to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.000
make a deck with the same prompt,&nbsp;
and they rose to the challenge.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.040
The Trinket Mage made a Codie, Vociferous Codex&nbsp;
donate deck, where the goal is to donate a pesky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:38.000
permanents to opponents such as The Sibsig&nbsp;
Ceremony, Steel Golem, or even Codie itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.080
There’s also some amount of permanent swapping,&nbsp;
theft, and threaten effects here, and Birthing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.720
Ritual exists to sacrifice a threatened creature&nbsp;
before it could return to its owner’s control.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:52.320
3/3 Elk made a Bhaal, Lord of Murder counter&nbsp;
manipulation and graveyard nonsense deck. Birthing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:56.400
Ritual is great all around in this sort of&nbsp;
shell, Aether Vial can get combo pieces deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.200
for explosive turns, and The Sibsig Ceremony is&nbsp;
used alongside cheap colorless creatures to fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.360
Evolution Witness creature-looping combos. This&nbsp;
is a different approach than I took, and there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.400
less broad synergy with The Sibsig Ceremony but&nbsp;
much more explosive combo potential with it.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:15.440
Quips&amp;Guac also picked out a Forgotten Realms&nbsp;
god—Myrkul, Lord of Bones. This card is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.280
neat with Sibsig Ceremony because when it&nbsp;
destroys your creatures, they simply come back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.000
as enchantments. Myrkul also has an indestructible&nbsp;
clause, so there’s a decent chance of being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.920
to get it back out at some point if it gets&nbsp;
deleted with The Sibsig Ceremony on board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:32.240
and this deck packs a whole lot of ramp to&nbsp;
pay for expensive plays with its commander.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.920
Arcosa built a Tayam combo deck.&nbsp;
Aether Vial and Birthing Ritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.800
are solid here since a large portion&nbsp;
of the deck is 1-3 mana creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.560
and The Sibsig Ceremony helps out with&nbsp;
multiple of the deck’s combo lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.800
Gravecrawler definitely stands out as a card that&nbsp;
The Sibsig Ceremony really works well alongside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.000
since it provides a zombie and doubles the&nbsp;
amount of potential sac triggers per cast.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.240
Blood Artist built a Sultai midrange&nbsp;
deck around Teval, the Balanced Scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.600
This is a heavily creature-based deck to&nbsp;
support Birthing Ritual and Aether Vial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.920
and we can see a number of cool Sibsig&nbsp;
Ceremony interactions here—lots of sac outlets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:08.320
lots of ETBs and death abilities, a ton of&nbsp;
recursion for looping ETB and death abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.480
and a number of leaving-graveyard payoffs&nbsp;
for generating value in the process.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.800
Thanks to these five for taking a crack at this,&nbsp;
I love all the cool ideas here. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.960
I’m sure there are plenty of&nbsp;
other neat ideas out there too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:23.200
let me know in the comments if you have&nbsp;
any ideas. I do actually read them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:26.080
and I love hearing all sorts of&nbsp;
creative thoughts from people.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.920
With that said, thanks for watching, and&nbsp;
thanks to Archidekt for supporting the channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.840
I’m always so glad to be an Archidekt user when&nbsp;
I’m building highly conceptual decks like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:38.000
due to all of its great features for analyzing&nbsp;
what I’m building. With a 2 second little search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:39.760
I can isolate the spirits with the word&nbsp;
“die” in their text on my screen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.600
look at just those cards to see what’s&nbsp;
there, both in my mainboard and in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

